  "query_preview": "..Strong high pressure is settling across the Desert \nSouthwest this weekend and will continue to influence our weather \nover the coming week. \n\nThe trough over the western US that has brought us some gusty winds, \nhas started to lift to the north as high pressure establishes itself \nacross the region. The high is slowly sinking to the south and the \nreturn flow around it will drag in some moisture from a decaying \ntropical system over the Pacific. The mid-levels will moisten first \nresulting in decent cloud cover across the region. CAMs do show that \nsome light showers may develop by early morning but would be very \nlight. Additionally, as we go into the early afternoon, we will see \nsome slight chances for showers and thunderstorms. While not all \nareas will see precipitation, some virga showers and sprinkles will \nremain possible. Any more robust showers or storms that develop may \nproduce outflow gusts up to 35 mph, mainly during the afternoon or \nearly evening. As the high shifts slightly west for Sunday, we will \ncontinue to have some moisture over the region. The best chances \nlook to be across the higher terrain with most of the activity \nremaining high-based. \n\nAs we head through the week, the high will strengthen and push \nwestward. This will limit moisture advection, especially across \nwestern portions of the forecast area, so any chances for showers \nwill be mostly limited to the White Mountains with some isolated \nchances along the Mogollon Rim through the week. The high slides \nback to the east toward the end of the week which may end up being \nslightly more favorable for us to see an increase in moisture. \nOverall, chances for widespread storms looks marginal and most of \nthe expected activity looks to only bring some light precipitation \namounts to the region.",
